Little Nick’s official backstory is classified because, according to Little Nick, “history is written by people above countertop height.”

Nobody actually knows when he appeared.

There are records of a tiny man resembling Nicolas Cage as far back as 1987, but every photograph is slightly blurry and every witness describes him doing something unnecessarily intense. In one picture he’s standing inside a punch bowl wearing a tuxedo. In another he appears to be riding a Roomba through the background of a Senate hearing.

Little Nick insists these are “unrelated operations.”

Regular Nicolas Cage first encountered him in 2004 while filming a movie in Prague. Cage returned to his hotel room at 2:13 a.m. and found an eight-inch version of himself sitting on the minibar, smoking an unlit cocktail straw and staring at a map of Europe.

Cage froze.

Little Nick looked up.

“Close the door.”

Cage closed the door.

Neither of them ever questioned why.

Little Nick then informed him that somewhere during the production of National Treasure, Cage had “disturbed the narrative substrate.”

“What does that mean?”

“It means you played Nicolas Cage too hard.”

“I wasn’t playing Nicolas Cage.”

Little Nick slowly looked at him.

“Exactly.”

Apparently, there is a theoretical maximum amount of Nicolas Cage that reality can contain in one human-sized region. During one particularly intense take, Cage exceeded what Little Nick calls the Cage Limit.

Reality corrected the imbalance by budding.

Like yeast.

Except Nicolas Cage.

Little Nick simply fell out of the universe behind a prop bookshelf.

He arrived fully dressed, fully literate, furious, and already owing somebody forty dollars.

He also has memories that regular Cage does not.

Little Nick remembers being a helicopter pilot.

He remembers marrying a countess.

He remembers an incident in Nevada he refers to only as “The Bees, But Worse.”

He claims he was once six feet tall.

Nobody believes this.

Jessica once asked him, “So are you a clone?”

Little Nick said, “Insulting.”

“A son?”

“Biologically impossible.”

“A hallucination?”

“Not according to my credit score.”

Regular Cage leaned over.

“You have a credit score?”

Little Nick stared into the middle distance.

“Not anymore.”

There is also one extremely important rule:

Little Nick cannot be photographed while regular Nicolas Cage is asleep.

Nobody knows why.

They tried once.

The photograph came out showing Little Nick standing beside the sleeping Cage—but behind Little Nick was another, even smaller Nicolas Cage.

About an inch tall.

Watching them.

Nobody has mentioned the photograph since.

Except Little Nick.

Sometimes, around 3 a.m., he’ll quietly say:

“Smaller Nick knows.”

Jessica: “Knows what?”

Little Nick:

“What I did.”

And that, apparently, is why Future Cage refused to explain why he’s small.